INTRODUCTORY NOTE TO RULE 48
Rules 48 sets the limits on jury size in federal civil trials. It takes account of the constitutional limits as described by Colgrove, but it also provides specifics about the maximum sizes of the jury and the requirement of a unanimous verdict.

Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 48
Number of Jurors; Verdict; Polling

(a) Number of Jurors. A jury must begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members * * * .

(b) Verdict. Unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous and must be returned by a jury of at least 6 members.

(c) Polling. After a verdict is returned but before the jury is discharged, the court must on a party’s request, or may on its own, poll the jurors individually. If the poll reveals a lack of unanimity or lack
